Better yet (though I didn't learn this until later), they'll use a
Shield of Reflection, which means you could equip it with a "oLS instead
(I rarely end up using them myself). Some experimentation shows that
Ki-rin will use a shield, helm, amulet, boots, and gloves (how those
last two work I have no idea), but no rings, cloaks, or suits. Once
fully equipped and pumped with a few extra !oGL, my ki-rin was lvl 29,
232 HP, AC -23, and essentially indestructible. The only, and I mean
only, thing that hurt him was attacking fire elementals in the Planes (I
ascended the Knight yesterday, BTW). That was a little dicey. Probably
the best way to deal with it would be to keep a couple extra full
healing pots around and dismount occasionally to feed them to it, but
that can be difficult when there are so many elementals around. Or
maybe there's a way to strike your steed with a wielded potion? I stuck
it out and relied on my extra healing spells and his own healing, but
that was maybe a little foolhardy. 

The other minor vulnerability of ki-rin is level-draining. I found too
late an old post here where somebody had the idea of using a cornuthaum
for their pet/steed for the ## cancellation factor. Unfortunately, I had
left a couple of pointy hats on the ground earlier in the game because I
didn't see the use for them. Great idea, in any event, though the
image of a great mythical unicorn/lion beast wearing a pointy wizard
hat is comical to say the least. In any event, I don't see that as much
of a limiting factor - the ki-rin will gain back the level quickly
enough so long as you haven't pushed it past base*1.5 with !oGL. So I
just held off on those until I got back to my base on the way back up
with the Amulet. After that point, there aren't any more drainers and
the mount certainly doesn't need the extra levels just for Rodney and
the Sanctum. Also interesting was the fact that the ki-rin will not wear
metallic armor - presumably because it's a spell caster, but maybe
because it has a hard time fitting metal plates over the pointy, horsey
bits. :)

The only other learning experience I had as far as mounts were
concerned was the possibility of getting a cursed saddle. That kind of
sucks, though the only reason I had to dismount after the Amulet was to
feed Nobunaga his !oGL. If I had known about the cornuthaum trick, I
would probably just level him up before going for the Amulet, and then
never get off again.

Overall, I would say that if any character capable of basic riding skill
has the fortune of finding either a saddle or a ki-rin in the main
dungeon that using a ki-rin mount would be worth a wish over speed
boots, given that your movement ends up being 2/1. For a knight,
it's a no-brainer. Had I recognized the danger that fire elementals
posed, I would have kept my attack spell skill at basic in order to get
the double damage on Cone of Cold from the Mirror just for them.

So there's my ki-rin treatise, I guess. It was a pretty fun game. I
think I'll have to play another Knight sometime soon to get a little bit
better picture of the challenge - the extra /oW made things a little
ridiculous this time around.

